If none of these conditions are met, the function enters a loop that continues until `n` reaches or exceeds 1. Inside this loop, the function calculates the next power of 3 (`powerOfThree`) and compares it with `n`. If they match, then `n` is a power of 3 and the function returns `true`. If `powerOfThree` is greater than `n`, then `n` cannot be a power of 3 and the function breaks out of the loop and returns `false`.

The time complexity of this function is O(log(n)), since it performs a constant number of iterations for each power of 3 between 1 and n. The space complexity is also O(1), as it only uses a few variables to keep track of the current value of `n` and the loop counter.

